    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "From a scientific and public health perspective, the core logic often relies on the concept of the \"harm principle,\" which posits that individual freedom can be legitimately restricted to prevent direct harm to others. Ethically, they often invoke a utilitarian framework, arguing that the greatest good for the greatest number justifies mandatory measures when disease risks are high, thus prioritizing community welfare. They also point to immunology, explaining that widespread immunity is a biological necessity for controlling epidemics, making mandates a necessary public health intervention rather than merely a suggestion.",
      "source": "target",
